Isothermal vs. Adiabatic Process | P-V Diagrams & Work Done Tricks | JEE NEET Physics,"Stop confusing the crucial differences between Isothermal and Adiabatic processes! In this video, we reveal the ultimate 'Slope Secret' Trick to instantly remember their P-V diagrams, work done formulas, and key characteristics.

Concepts Covered:
⚡ Case 1 - Isothermal Process - The Smooth Curve
  For an Isothermal process, temperature (T) remains constant. Pressure (P) and Volume (V) are inversely proportional (PV = constant).
  *   P-V Diagram: Rectangular Hyperbola (smoother slope)
  *   Work Done (W): nRT ln(Vf/Vi) or nRT ln(Pi/Pf)
  *   Key Feature: Internal energy (ΔU) is zero.
  *   Trick: 'Iso-Temp, Smooth Slope, log Work.'

⚡ Case 2 - Adiabatic Process - The Steep Decline
  For an Adiabatic process, no heat exchange (Q=0) occurs with the surroundings. PV^γ = constant.
  *   P-V Diagram: Steeper curve than isothermal.
  *   Work Done (W): (P1V1 - P2V2) / (γ - 1) or nR(T1 - T2) / (γ - 1)
  *   Key Feature: Internal energy changes directly due to work done (ΔU = -W).
  *   Trick: 'Adi-No Heat, Steep Slope, Gamma Work.'

⚡ Why the difference?
  The difference lies in how internal energy changes and the presence or absence of heat exchange. Adiabatic processes involve faster changes where heat transfer doesn't have time to occur, leading to a steeper pressure drop for a given volume change compared to isothermal processes where temperature is held constant by heat exchange.

