☀️LIVE: Real-Time Solar Flare & CME Activity Monitor | Space Weather Alerts 🛰️,"This live stream displays a real-time solar activity detection and space weather monitoring system by Cosmic Pulse Labs, highlighting significant events from our Sun.

 Audio Alerts: Each sound indicates a potential solar event (e.g., M-class or X-class flare, significant Coronal Mass Ejection).
 Map Visualization: Animated graphics visualize solar flares, coronal mass ejections (CMEs), and sunspot regions. A dynamic Earth impact prediction model shows potential geomagnetic storm effects and aurora forecasts.
 Recent Activity: Displays all major solar events detected exclusively by this system in the past 24 hours. Events appear as color-coded indicators—green for minor flares, shifting to yellow, orange, and red based on estimated intensity (e.g., C, M, X class).
 Event Details: For each event, the stream provides rapid estimates including UTC date and time, event type, class/intensity estimation, and potential Earth impact.
️ Progressive Mapping: During significant events, the view may zoom into specific solar regions or Earth's magnetosphere to illustrate impact.

⚠️ Disclaimer – Preliminary Data
Solar information provided on this live stream (flare class, CME velocity, impact predictions, etc.) are automatically calculated and exclusively based on the analysis performed by the Cosmic Pulse Labs monitoring system, using the data sources available to it and has not been reviewed by human experts. No manual verification is performed.
These data are preliminary and may contain inaccuracies, including incorrect classifications, timings, or impact predictions.
The information is supplied for informational purposes only and does not constitute an official alert or communication from space weather authorities.
Users are reminded that all use of this information is at their own risk. The live stream operator and data providers accept no responsibility for any loss, damage, or decisions made based on the use of these data.
Please refer to official sources (such as NOAA's Space Weather Prediction Center or NASA) for confirmed and authoritative space weather information.

