Biomimetic Exoskeleton: Generative Design for 3D Printed Robotic Modules (Master's Thesis),"Biomimetic Exoskeleton: Master's Thesis in Advanced Industrial Design - 3D Printed Robotic Module Showcase. This project delves into the application of biomimicry and generative design principles to create highly efficient and aesthetically integrated exoskeleton modules for robotics. Drawing inspiration from natural structures like insect exoskeletons and bone trabeculae, I leveraged advanced computational design tools to optimize structural integrity and reduce material usage while maintaining a unique, organic aesthetic.

My focus was on demonstrating how complex, functional forms can emerge from iterative generative processes, specifically tailored for additive manufacturing. This involved exploring topology optimization and lattice structure generation, enabling the creation of lightweight yet incredibly strong components that integrate form and function seamlessly.

Design Focus: Biomimetic Form Generation & Additive Manufacturing Optimization

Key Features: High strength-to-weight ratio, integrated internal channels for wiring/sensors, modular self-aligning magnetic assembly, and adaptable organic aesthetics.

Assembly Note: Primarily utilizes self-aligning magnetic connectors and interlocking features, with minimal external fasteners.

Focus: The synergistic intersection of nature-inspired design, computational engineering, and 3D printing for future robotic systems.


