AI and the Truth Crisis: Navigating Synthetic Realities (The 2024 Innovate & Inform Lecture),"The Institute for Digital Ethics (IDE) presents Dr. Evelyn Reed, a leading expert in computational social science, as she explores the burgeoning challenges posed by artificial intelligence to public discourse and truth. Her lecture, 'AI and the Truth Crisis: Navigating Synthetic Realities,' will be held on Thursday, October 24, at 6:30 p.m. in the Grand Auditorium. The event is open to the public and aims to foster critical discussion on these pressing issues. Drawing from her latest research on generative AI and deepfakes, Dr. Reed will dissect the mechanisms through which AI can manipulate information, create convincing synthetic media, and potentially erode trust in traditional news sources. She will offer insights into the cognitive biases that make individuals susceptible to AI-generated disinformation and discuss potential societal impacts on elections, public health, and social cohesion. Dr. Reed will also propose strategies for digital literacy and policy interventions to safeguard democratic processes in an increasingly AI-driven information landscape. Dr. Reed is the Director of the Center for AI and Society at Stanford University and a distinguished fellow at the Ethics in AI Council. Her work frequently appears in Nature Human Behaviour and the Journal of Communication. This lecture is part of the Innovate & Inform Lecture Series, dedicated to exploring cutting-edge topics at the intersection of technology, ethics, and society. The program is co-sponsored by the Department of Cognitive Science and the School of Law at Stanford.",The Algorithmic Agora,85
