1st Year Physics | Lecture 17 | Quantum Tunneling & Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle | Dr. Elena Petrova,"1st Year Physics Lecture | April 5th | Quantum Tunneling & Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle
Cosmic Classroom Online Series | Dr. Elena Petrova

In this insightful 1st Year Physics lecture, Dr. Elena Petrova demystifies the fundamental concepts of Quantum Tunneling and Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle for intermediate students. These cornerstones of quantum mechanics are crucial for understanding the behavior of particles at the atomic and subatomic levels, providing a deeper insight into the modern world of physics.

The lecture begins by exploring Quantum Tunneling, explaining how particles can seemingly 'pass through' energy barriers, a phenomenon impossible in classical physics but essential for processes like nuclear fusion in the sun and scanning tunneling microscopy. Students will gain a clear understanding of the wave-particle duality that underpins this quantum effect.

Following this, Dr. Petrova delves into Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle, elucidating the inherent limits to the precision with which certain pairs of physical properties, such as position and momentum, can be known simultaneously. This discussion will highlight the probabilistic nature of quantum reality and its implications for measurement and observation.

This session is part of the Cosmic Classroom Online Series, where Dr. Elena Petrova offers comprehensive and conceptually rich physics lectures designed to build a strong foundation in quantum physics for 1st Year students. The goal is to prepare students for academic challenges and inspire a deeper appreciation for the mysteries of the universe.

