Seminar: Decoding the Cosmos with Warm Dark Matter Candidates,"Join us for a deep dive into the fascinating world of Warm Dark Matter (WDM) candidates. While Cold Dark Matter (CDM) remains the standard model, tensions with small-scale structure formation and direct detection efforts have spurred interest in alternative models. This seminar will explore the theoretical motivations for WDM, focusing on sterile neutrinos and gravitinos, and discuss their distinct cosmological signatures. We will examine how WDM impacts structure formation, from the suppression of small-scale power spectra to the properties of dwarf galaxies, and review current observational constraints from the Lyman-alpha forest, galaxy surveys, and cosmic microwave background anisotropies. The talk will also touch upon the experimental efforts to detect WDM particles and the challenges involved. Our speaker will present recent work on refining WDM models to reconcile them with the latest astrophysical data, offering insights into how these elusive particles might shape the universe we observe. This discussion aims to provide a comprehensive overview of the WDM paradigm, highlighting its potential to resolve long-standing cosmological puzzles.",Stanford Cosmology Lab,105
