He Saved Millions with a Simple Idea. The Medical World Tried to Destroy Him.,"Ignaz Semmelweis, a Hungarian physician, made a life-saving discovery in the 1840s: doctors were unknowingly spreading deadly diseases, particularly puerperal fever, by not washing their hands between autopsies and examining patients. His simple solution – hand disinfection with chlorinated lime – drastically reduced mortality rates. Yet, the medical establishment vehemently rejected his findings, viewing them as an insult to their integrity. He was ridiculed, ostracized, and ultimately committed to an asylum, dying heartbroken without witnessing the global adoption of his crucial insight. His legacy, however, paved the way for modern hygiene and saved millions of lives. #IgnazSemmelweis #Handwashing #MedicalHistory #PublicHealth #UnsungHero #Science #ForgottenGeniuses #shorts Subscribe for more untold stories of those who changed the world against all odds!",Unseen Pioneers,620
