Rosalind Franklin: The Unsung Heroine Who Unlocked DNA's Secret,"Rosalind Franklin, born in London in 1920, was a brilliant chemist and X-ray crystallographer whose meticulous work was crucial to understanding the molecular structures of DNA, RNA, viruses, coal, and graphite. Educated at Newnham College, Cambridge, Franklin's early career focused on the physical chemistry of carbons. Her most significant work began at King's College London, where she captured ""Photo 51,"" an iconic X-ray diffraction image that provided critical insights into DNA's helical structure. Despite her pivotal data being shared without her explicit knowledge or consent, leading to Watson and Crick's double helix model, her contributions were often downplayed during her lifetime. Tragically, Franklin died young at 37 from ovarian cancer, never receiving the Nobel Prize for her work, which was awarded to Watson, Crick, and Wilkins years later. Her legacy, however, has grown, recognizing her as an essential figure in molecular biology and a symbol of overlooked scientific genius. #rosalindfranklin #dnastructure #xraycrystallography #historyofscience #womenscientists #molecularbiology #scientificpioneers #unsungheroes",Scientific Legacies,120
