TESS Exoplanet Light Curves: Visualizing the Latest Transit Data | Kepler-186f [8:30],"Explore the fascinating world of exoplanets through advanced data visualization of TESS (Transiting Exoplanet Survey Satellite) light curves. This video showcases the intricate transit data for a specific exoplanetary system, demonstrating how faint dips in stellar brightness reveal distant worlds. Our custom pipeline processes raw telemetry to uncover planetary transits with high precision.

🔭 TELESCOPE MISSION:
• TESS (Transiting Exoplanet Survey Satellite) — Wide-field exoplanet transit photometry, 2-minute and 20-second cadences.
• Kepler (Kepler Space Telescope) — Precursor mission, high-precision photometry.

📊 DATA PROCESSING & VISUALIZATION PIPELINE:
Each target pixel file (TPF) and light curve is automatically processed with:
• Flux Detrending: Removal of instrumental and stellar variability (e.g., `wotan`, Savitzky-Golay filters).
• Phase Folding: Aligning multiple transits to a common epoch for enhanced signal.
• Transit Model Fitting: Using algorithms like `BATMAN` to characterize planetary parameters (radius, period, etc.).
• Periodogram Analysis: Identifying periodic signals to confirm planetary candidates.
• Interactive Light Curve Plots: High-resolution visualizations of stellar flux over time.
• Stellar Parameter Integration: Incorporating data from Gaia, TESS Input Catalog (TIC) for host star characterization.

🎥 VIDEO CREATION:
Videos feature dynamic plotting, synchronized animations highlighting transit events, and overlays of derived planetary parameters. Smooth transitions between raw data and processed light curves, with explanatory text overlays for key features.

💡 DATA SOURCE:
• Archive: MAST (Mikulski Archive for Space Telescopes) at STScI, TESS Science Office.
• Processing: Custom Python pipeline (`lightkurve`, `astropy`, `numpy`, `matplotlib`, `bokeh`).
• Data Level: Processed light curves and derived parameters (Level 2/3).

Subscribe for weekly deep dives into exoplanet discoveries and advanced astronomical data analysis!

