The Ghost in Your Genes: How the Black Death Still Shapes European DNA,"What happens when one of history's deadliest pandemics sweeps across a continent, wiping out a third of the population? The Black Death wasn't just a catastrophic event that reshaped societies; it acted as an intense evolutionary pressure, leaving an indelible mark on the human genome. In this video, we delve into groundbreaking scientific research that reveals how the survivors of the bubonic plague passed on crucial genetic adaptations to their descendants. We'll explore specific gene mutations—like the CCR5 delta 32 allele and others linked to immune response—that offered protection against the plague, and how these genes are still prevalent in European populations today. Discover how this medieval scourge inadvertently prepared some populations for future health challenges, and why understanding this ancient genetic legacy is vital for modern medicine. This isn't just history; it's the biological echoes of a plague that continue to resonate within us.SOURCES─────────────────────────────• Nature (2022) — Natural selection against plague in ancient Europeans (Galaway et al.)• Science (2014) — Genome-wide signatures of plague and smallpox resistance in Europeans• Max Planck Institute for Evolutionary Anthropology• Historical accounts of the Black Death's impact on European demography",GeneEchoes,34500
