Dr. Elara Vance - Machine Learning for Accelerated Quantum Discovery - Computational Frontiers Seminar,"Recorded 25 April 2027. Dr. Elara Vance from the Quantum Algorithms Lab presents 'Machine Learning for Accelerated Quantum Discovery' at the Computational Frontiers Institute's annual seminar series. Abstract: The field of quantum chemistry faces immense computational challenges when modeling complex molecular systems, which limits the discovery of new materials and pharmaceuticals. This presentation explores how advanced machine learning techniques are transforming quantum chemistry, enabling breakthroughs that were previously intractable. Dr. Vance will discuss the integration of deep learning, especially Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), with quantum mechanical simulations to accelerate the prediction of molecular properties, optimize reaction pathways, and facilitate the inverse design of novel compounds. The talk will begin with an overview of the current computational bottlenecks in quantum chemistry and how classical approximations often fall short. We will then delve into specific applications of ML, including: 1. High-throughput screening: Using ML models trained on quantum data to rapidly predict properties like electronic structure, thermodynamic stability, and catalytic activity for vast chemical spaces. 2. Molecular dynamics acceleration: Employing Neural Network Potentials (NNPs) to drastically reduce the computational cost of simulating atomic interactions, allowing for longer and more accurate molecular dynamics simulations. 3. Inverse design: Leveraging generative AI and reinforcement learning to design molecules with desired functionalities, moving beyond traditional trial-and-error approaches. 4. Quantum Machine Learning: A brief look at emerging methods that utilize quantum computing principles to enhance ML algorithms for chemical problems, offering a glimpse into future possibilities. Dr. Vance will provide accessible examples, including the application of GNNs to predict the binding affinity of drug candidates and the use of reinforcement learning in optimizing synthetic routes. The presentation will also touch upon the challenges of data generation, model interpretability, and the need for robust validation in this interdisciplinary domain. This work is supported by the National Science Foundation's 'AI for Scientific Discovery' initiative and the Advanced Materials Research Consortium.",Computational Frontiers Institute,920
