UNCOVERED! North Korea's Secret Invasion Tunnels Under DMZ Revealed #shorts,"UNCOVERED! North Korea's Secret Invasion Tunnels Under DMZ Revealed #shorts

The Demilitarized Zone (DMZ) separating North and South Korea is one of the most heavily fortified borders in the world, but beneath its surface lies a hidden threat: North Korea's secret invasion tunnels. For decades, North Korea has been digging a vast network of underground tunnels designed to facilitate a surprise invasion of the South.

So far, four such tunnels have been officially discovered by South Korea, but intelligence agencies believe many more exist. The first tunnel was found in 1974, just 65 kilometers northeast of Seoul. It was large enough for a full regiment of soldiers to pass through per hour, carrying light weaponry. Subsequent tunnels were discovered in 1975, 1978, and 1990. The third tunnel, found near Panmunjom, is particularly significant as it was designed to allow 30,000 troops per hour, along with field artillery, to cross into South Korea.

These tunnels are often dug through hard granite, lined with concrete, and equipped with rails for troop and equipment movement. North Korea's official stance is that these tunnels are coal mines, a claim consistently refuted by forensic evidence found within the tunnels. The existence of these tunnels highlights North Korea's long-standing strategy of unconventional warfare and its continuous threat to regional stability. Their discovery caused major international concern and remains a point of tension between the two Koreas.

