Climate Resilience in Small Island Developing States – Interview with Dr. Anya Sharma (University of Oxford),"Dr. Anya Sharma (University of Oxford) in conversation with Dr. Leo Chen (Global Nexus Institute)

- How can traditional ecological knowledge be integrated into modern climate adaptation strategies for SIDS?
- What challenges do SIDS face in accessing and utilizing international climate finance?
- What role does community-led research play in building effective and sustainable climate resilience?

In this episode, Dr. Anya Sharma from the Environmental Change Institute at the University of Oxford introduces us to the complex and urgent challenges of climate change adaptation in Small Island Developing States (SIDS).

Drawing on her extensive fieldwork and policy research, Dr. Sharma elaborates on how geographical vulnerability, socio-economic factors, and historical legacies shape resilience efforts, particularly among coastal communities in the Pacific and Caribbean. Within this framework, she highlights the critical significance of innovative strategies such as community-based adaptation and the preservation of indigenous knowledge, demonstrating how local participation and traditional practices can substantially enhance climate resilience and sustainable development. At the same time, she reflects on the importance of multi-stakeholder engagement, ethical considerations in climate research, and the challenges of bridging the gap between scientific findings and actionable policy across diverse island contexts. The conversation further underscores the value of interdisciplinary approaches, alongside international cooperation, in developing equitable and culturally sensitive adaptation interventions.

Join us as we explore how climate resilience efforts reveal broader dynamics of global equity and environmental justice, and what they teach us about addressing the planetary crisis!

For Dr. Anya Sharma’s Academic Profile:
https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Anya-Sharma

