The Idea That Got a 'C' Grade and Built a Billion-Dollar Empire: The Fred Smith Story (FedEx Founder),"“What if your groundbreaking idea was dismissed as 'impractical' by your professors? What if that idea later revolutionized an entire industry?” Story In the late 1960s, Fred Smith, a student at Yale University, wrote a term paper for an economics class. His idea? A reliable overnight delivery service, specializing in small, time-sensitive packages. He envisioned a central hub where all packages would converge, be sorted, and then flown out to their destinations overnight. His professor's response? A C grade, with a note suggesting the idea wasn't feasible. But Smith believed in his vision. After serving in the Marines and working for a while, he took his “impractical” idea and poured his inheritance and venture capital into it. In 1973, he launched Federal Express (FedEx). The early days were tough, with the company often on the brink of bankruptcy. At one point, he even used his personal poker winnings to cover fuel costs. Today, FedEx is a global powerhouse, delivering millions of packages daily and connecting economies worldwide. And the idea that received a mere 'C' grade... reshaped global logistics and proved that true innovation often looks ""impractical"" at first. “Don't let a grade or someone else's skepticism define the potential of your vision.” “I am Alex. Keep believing in your vision.”",Alex's Insights,620
