How Your Kidneys Filter Blood | Nephrons & Waste Removal Explained #shorts #kidneyhealth #science,"Every drop of blood in your body gets cleaned multiple times a day. But how do your kidneys perform this vital purification act?

In this video, we dive into the microscopic marvel of your kidneys, explaining the incredible process that removes waste and keeps your body balanced.

🔬 The Blood Filtration Process Explained

• NEPHRONS – The kidney's tiny, but mighty, filtering units. Each kidney contains over a million of these, working tirelessly.

• GLOMERULUS – A network of tiny blood vessels within each nephron where initial filtration occurs, pushing waste and excess water out of the blood.

• TUBULES – Long, winding tubes where essential substances (like water, glucose, and salts) are reabsorbed back into the blood, while waste products are concentrated into urine.

• WASTE REMOVAL – Urea, toxins, and excess minerals are collected and transported to the bladder for excretion, preventing harmful buildup in your system.

The Takeaway:
Your kidneys process about 1 liter of blood every minute! They are essential for maintaining fluid balance, blood pressure, and detoxifying your body. Protect your kidneys—they're your body's unsung heroes.

Save this video to understand the amazing work happening inside you! 💪

