The Forgotten Genius Who Revolutionized How We See | Ibn al-Haytham's Optical Legacy,"Before Galileo. Before Newton. Before Bacon. There was Ibn al-Haytham (965-1040 CE).
The brilliant 10th-century polymath from Basra who truly founded the science of optics and pioneered the modern scientific method.

👁️ His Revolutionary Contributions:
• Revolutionized the understanding of vision: Light enters the eye, rather than emanating from it (overthrowing 1,500 years of Greek theory).
• Invented the Camera Obscura (pinhole camera), a crucial step in understanding light and projection.
• Pioneered experimental physics: Emphasized observation, hypothesis, and rigorous testing – centuries before Francis Bacon.
• Developed laws of reflection and refraction through meticulous experiments.
• His monumental work, “Kitāb al-Manāẓir” (Book of Optics), became a cornerstone of scientific inquiry for centuries.
• Conducted the first systematic studies of binocular vision and atmospheric refraction.

📚 His legacy—and how it was erased:

His books were translated into Latin as ""Alhazen's Opticae Thesaurus"" in the 12th century.
European scholars like Roger Bacon, Witelo, Johannes Kepler, and even Isaac Newton's predecessors drew heavily from his work, building directly upon his discoveries and methods.
Yet, his original contributions and his name are rarely taught as the foundational elements they truly are.
The scientific method, often credited to figures like Francis Bacon in the 17th century, was explicitly outlined and practiced by Ibn al-Haytham centuries prior.

💡 The cruelest irony:

The foundational principles of experimental science, which he so meticulously developed, are often attributed to later Western thinkers, leaving the true pioneer largely unknown.
His brilliance became the unacknowledged bedrock for centuries of optical and scientific advancement, shaping how we understand light, vision, and scientific inquiry itself.

This is the story physics textbooks often overlook.

⏱️ Timestamps:
0:00 - Who truly founded optics and the scientific method?
0:15 - Ibn al-Haytham's revolutionary discoveries
0:30 - The ""Alhazen"" translations
0:45 - How others got the credit for his methods
1:00 - The unacknowledged bedrock of modern science
1:15 - Why this forgotten history matters today

📚 Sources:
- ""Ibn al-Haytham: The First Scientist"" - Bradley Steffens
- ""Science and Islam"" - Ehsan Masood
- ""The Book of Optics"" (Kitāb al-Manāẓir) - Ibn al-Haytham (original and translated editions)

🔬 Physics students, history buffs: Was Ibn al-Haytham ever mentioned in your curriculum?

