PIM Architectures Unveiled: A Tutorial on Simulating Processing-in-Memory with Gem5 and PIM-Bench (HPCA'27 Workshop),"The advent of the 'memory wall' and the increasing energy cost of data movement between CPU and main memory necessitate novel computing paradigms. Processing-in-Memory (PIM) architectures offer a promising solution by bringing computation closer to data, drastically reducing data transfer overheads and unlocking new levels of performance and energy efficiency for data-intensive applications.

This tutorial, held in conjunction with HPCA'27, provides a comprehensive introduction to PIM architectures, their design principles, and, critically, how to effectively simulate and evaluate them using state-of-the-art tools. We will dive deep into: 

*   **Fundamentals of PIM**: Understanding different PIM paradigms, from near-memory processing to in-memory computing.
*   **Simulation with Gem5**: Hands-on sessions demonstrating how to extend and use the Gem5 simulator framework to model PIM-enabled systems.
*   **PIM-Bench Benchmark Suite**: A detailed exploration of PIM-Bench, a collection of benchmarks designed to characterize and evaluate PIM architectures across various application domains.
*   **Performance and Energy Analysis**: Methodologies for analyzing the performance, power consumption, and thermal implications of PIM designs.

This workshop is ideal for researchers, Ph.D. students, and industry professionals interested in exploring the cutting edge of memory systems, computer architecture, and high-performance computing.

Workshop Website: https://events.futuresyslab.edu/hpca27-pimsim/
Gem5 PIM Extensions GitHub: https://github.com/FutureSysLab/gem5-pim
PIM-Bench GitHub: https://github.com/FutureSysLab/PIM-Bench",Future Computing Architectures,750
