Post-Quantum Cryptography: Securing Our Digital Future Against Quantum Threats,"In this illuminating presentation, Dr. Anya Sharma, a leading expert in cryptographic security, delves into the critical field of Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC). As quantum computing capabilities advance, the security of our current digital infrastructure, reliant on classical cryptographic algorithms, faces an existential threat. This talk, extracted from the 'Future of Information Security Summit', outlines the urgent need for new cryptographic solutions that can withstand the computational power of future quantum computers. Dr. Sharma explains the fundamental principles behind PQC, contrasting it with Quantum Key Distribution (QKD) and detailing why both are crucial, yet distinct, components of a comprehensive quantum-safe strategy. She explores the different families of PQC algorithms, including lattice-based, code-based, and multivariate polynomial cryptography, discussing their strengths, weaknesses, and current standardization efforts by organizations like NIST. The presentation emphasizes the immense challenge of migrating global digital systems to quantum-resistant standards, touching upon the complexities for governments, financial institutions, and critical infrastructure. It highlights the economic and societal implications of failing to prepare for the 'Q-Day' – the moment when quantum computers can break widely used encryption. This session serves as a vital call to action, illustrating that the transition to PQC is not a distant concern but an ongoing race against time to safeguard sensitive data and ensure the long-term integrity of our digital world. Dr. Sharma's insights provide a concrete roadmap for understanding and implementing the next generation of cryptographic security.",Quantum Shield Labs,7
