"Earth's Aurora 24-Hour Timelapse | Oxygen & Nitrogen Emissions | From Orbit (July 10, 2026)","A 24-hour time-lapse of the breathtaking Aurora Borealis and Australis, captured from low Earth orbit. This visualization highlights the distinct spectral emissions from excited oxygen and nitrogen atoms, revealing the dynamic interplay between solar wind particles and Earth's magnetosphere. Each color represents a specific atomic process, offering a unique perspective on our planet's atmospheric light show.

🌌 EMISSION SCIENCE
The vibrant colors of the aurora are generated by energized particles colliding with atmospheric gases:
• 557.7 nm (Oxygen) — Green — Common and bright, from O atoms at lower altitudes.
• 630.0 nm (Oxygen) — Red — Higher altitude O atoms, often seen as faint upper edges.
• 427.8 nm (Nitrogen) — Blue/Violet — Nitrogen molecule ionization and excitation, less common but spectacular.

🛰️ VISUALIZATION & DATA
• Satellite: International Space Station (ISS) / DLR Earth Sensing Imaging Spectrometer (DESIS) or similar orbital instrument for spectral imaging.
• Data: Raw spectral data processed to isolate and enhance specific emission lines.
• Visualization: Custom color mapping to represent scientific data accurately, enhancing the visual distinction between emission types.
• Pipeline: Python • NumPy • SciPy • Matplotlib • OpenCV

