I Built an Automated XSS Scanner (Reflected → DOM-based),"In this video, I demonstrate how I built a custom Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Automation Tool and used it to identify and exploit various XSS vulnerabilities on a target web application, from basic reflected XSS to advanced stored and DOM-based attacks.

Instead of relying only on automated tools like Burp Suite Scanner or ZAP, this project shows how security researchers and penetration testers can build their own automation scripts to gain a deeper understanding of XSS mechanics and detect harder-to-find vulnerabilities.

The demonstration is performed on OWASP Juice Shop and DVWA (Damn Vulnerable Web Application) for educational and security research purposes.

➡ Time Stamps:
00:00 - Introduction
00:25 - Project Showcase
00:30 - Phase 1: Setup & Installation
03:45 - Phase 2: Basic Reflected XSS Detection
06:10 - Phase 3: Stored XSS Exploitation Automation
08:55 - Phase 4: Advanced DOM-based XSS Scanning
12:05 - Outro & Summary of Benefits.

💡 What You Will Learn
In this video, we cover:
• 💻 Understanding Cross-Site Scripting basics
• ⚙️ Building a custom automation tool for XSS
• 🔎 Detecting XSS vulnerabilities automatically
• 🌐 Exploiting Reflected, Stored, and DOM-based XSS
• 🛡️ Understanding how attackers bypass basic defenses like WAFs
• 🍪 Extracting cookie information and potential session hijacking
• 📈 Improving your web application security skills

This project demonstrates how penetration testers can automate repetitive exploitation tasks while understanding the underlying logic behind XSS.

🛠️ Technologies Used
• 🐍 Python
• 🌐 HTTP Requests Library
• 📝 XSS Payloads
• 🛒 OWASP Juice Shop
• ⚠️ DVWA (Damn Vulnerable Web Application)
• 🐧 Parrot OS & Kali Linux
• ⚙️ Custom Automation Script

⚡ XSS Techniques Covered

➡️ Reflected XSS
Simple payload testing where user input is immediately returned by the web server in an unsafe manner, allowing malicious script execution.

➡️ Stored XSS
The automation tool identifies and exploits scenarios where malicious scripts are permanently stored on the target server (e.g., in a database) and are later retrieved and executed by other users.

➡️ DOM-based XSS
The script analyzes client-side JavaScript code to find vulnerabilities where the malicious payload executes as a result of modifying the Document Object Model (DOM) environment in the victim's browser.

Why Build Your Own XSS Tool?
Tools like Burp Suite Scanner and ZAP are powerful, but understanding how to build automation helps you:
• understand how XSS works internally
• improve penetration testing skills
• bypass custom filters and Web Application Firewalls (WAFs)
• automate repetitive discovery and exploitation tasks
• become a better security researcher

⚠️ Ethical Disclaimer
This video is created strictly for educational and cybersecurity learning purposes.
All demonstrations are performed on intentionally vulnerable environments like OWASP Juice Shop and DVWA.
🚫 Never attempt to exploit systems without explicit authorization.
Unauthorized attacks on real systems are illegal and unethical.
Always practice responsible and ethical hacking.

