Why Onions Make You Cry (The Science of Tears) #chemistry,"The Science of Onion Tears
It's a universal kitchen experience: slicing an onion and suddenly, tears are streaming down your face. But why does this happen? It's not just a random inconvenience; it's a fascinating biological defense mechanism!

Here's the ""Tear-Jerking Truth"":
• Enzyme Release: When you cut into an onion, you damage its cells, releasing an enzyme called alliinase.
• Precursor Reaction: Alliinase then reacts with sulfur compounds (specifically, S-alk(en)yl-L-cysteine sulfoxides) naturally present in the onion. 
• Irritant Formation: This reaction rapidly produces a volatile gas known as syn-propanethial S-oxide. This airborne chemical is what floats up to your eyes, irritating your lacrimal glands and causing them to produce tears to wash it away.

Tips & Clarifications:
1. It's a Defense Mechanism: This chemical irritant is actually a clever way for the onion to deter pests and animals from eating it.
2. How to Fight Back: Want to reduce the tears? Try chilling the onion in the fridge or freezer for 15-30 minutes before cutting, using a very sharp knife (to reduce cell damage), cutting near a fan, or even wearing swim goggles!
3. Not All Onions Are Equal: The concentration of these sulfur compounds can vary between different onion varieties, which is why some onions make you cry more than others.",Daily Dose of Science,220
