CFD Masterclass: Lecture 1 - Fundamentals of Blood Flow Simulation in Vascular Networks,"Lecture Series Background: The BioFlow Dynamics Masterclass series was developed by Dr. Anya Sharma during the Spring of 2024 for advanced graduate students and postdoctoral researchers specializing in biomedical engineering and computational physiology. The primary goal is to equip participants with the theoretical foundations and practical skills to confidently apply Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) to complex biological systems, particularly focusing on cardiovascular mechanics. Building upon a decade of teaching advanced fluid dynamics and biomechanics, this course integrates cutting-edge research with hands-on software application. The series comprises 12 in-depth lectures, each accompanied by lecture notes, simulation files, and practical exercises. The examples primarily utilize open-source CFD platforms like OpenFOAM, alongside discussions of commercial alternatives, compatible with current stable versions. This series is designed for serious researchers and practitioners aiming to push the boundaries of medical device design, disease prognosis, and fundamental physiological understanding through advanced simulation. While these lectures are unedited, we encourage direct engagement. For any questions or discussions related to the content, please reach out to Dr. Sharma at ashrma.cfd@bioflowdyn.org. We hope you find this resource valuable. Dr. Anya Sharma May 15, 2024 ******** In this inaugural CFD Masterclass lecture, Dr. Anya Sharma introduces the philosophical and methodological underpinnings of applying CFD to biological systems. She emphasizes approaching CFD as a powerful research instrument, not merely a black box. The session delves into core governing equations, how CFD models fluid-structure interaction (FSI) in vascular walls, and its critical role in contemporary biomedical and clinical research. Examples span from patient-specific aneurysm modeling to optimizing prosthetic heart valve designs. The lecture also outlines the comprehensive course trajectory, covering everything from mesh generation for intricate geometries to advanced turbulence modeling and coupled multi-physics simulations. Recording date: Feb 12, 2024 Topics covered in Lecture 1: · Purpose of the Masterclass and learning objectives: Becoming proficient in biological CFD, understanding underlying fluid mechanics principles, and adapting/extending CFD tools for novel research questions (e.g., non-Newtonian blood models, cellular transport). · High-level course roadmap: Fundamental fluid dynamics for bio-systems, patient-specific geometry acquisition (CT/MRI), advanced meshing strategies, boundary conditions for pulsatile flow, OpenFOAM basics, FSI, multi-scale modeling, and AI integration in CFD. · CFD physics fundamentals: Focus on adapting Navier-Stokes equations for biological fluids (e.g., viscoelasticity of blood), mass conservation, energy equations, and the bio-rheological considerations that distinguish bio-CFD. · Modeling philosophy: Emphasizing CFD as a diagnostic and predictive tool, not a definitive truth, promoting critical assessment of assumptions, numerical errors, and the sensitivity of biological parameters. · CFD workflows and software: Command-line and graphical interfaces for OpenFOAM/Fluent, best practices for model setup, parallel computing, and post-processing for clinically relevant metrics. · Scripting and customization: Role of scripting (Python, C++) for automating workflows, implementing custom constitutive models for tissues, and developing novel analysis tools. · Fluid-Structure Interaction (FSI): Introduction to coupled FSI for understanding vessel wall mechanics, implant interactions, and the challenges of two-way coupling in complex biological geometries. · Advanced biological models: Overview of micro-scale blood rheology, particulate flow (e.g., drug delivery), and coupled thermo-fluid simulations for hyperthermia or cryotherapy. · Real-world and clinical examples: Case studies including coronary artery disease progression, cerebral aneurysm rupture risk assessment, optimizing ventricular assist devices, and designing drug-eluting stents. · Role of participant contributions: Encouraging students to bring their own research challenges for discussion and tailored guidance, fostering a collaborative learning environment. · Research directions for future PhD/Postdoc work: Highlighting emerging areas like AI-driven CFD, multi-scale and multi-physics modeling in personalized medicine, and uncertainty quantification in clinical predictions.",BioFlow Dynamics Research,387
