'The Elegant Geometry of Soap Films' Public Lecture by Dr. Anya Sharma,"Explore the beautiful and complex world hidden within simple soap bubbles and films. Dr. Anya Sharma demonstrates how these ephemeral structures naturally seek to minimize their surface area, revealing fundamental principles of geometry, calculus of variations, and even architecture. Through a series of captivating tabletop experiments using frames, wires, and specially formulated soap solutions, we'll uncover the mathematics behind minimal surfaces, Plateau's laws, and why bubbles are always round. Dr. Anya Sharma is a professor of applied mathematics at the University of Cambridge, specializing in fluid dynamics and material science. Her unique path began as a sculptor before she pursued a Ph.D. in theoretical physics, bringing an artist's eye to scientific inquiry. She is passionate about making advanced mathematical concepts accessible to a wider audience, frequently collaborating with science museums and educational platforms.",Cosmos & Logic Lectures,350
The Cosmic Origins of Earth's Water: It's Not What You Think,"Every drop of water you drink, every ocean wave, and every cloud in the sky has a story that begins billions of years ago, far beyond our planet. For a long time, scientists believed Earth's water came primarily from volcanic outgassing. But new evidence suggests a far more dramatic and distant journey. Imagine a young, scorching Earth, bombarded by a relentless shower of icy asteroids and comets – cosmic messengers carrying the building blocks of our oceans from the frigid outer reaches of the solar system. These ancient, frozen wanderers, rich in H2O, slammed into our nascent planet, slowly but surely filling the basins that would become our life-giving seas. So the next time you see a vast ocean, remember you're looking at a cosmic gift, delivered by countless impacts from space, making life on Earth possible. It's a testament to the incredible, violent, and beautiful processes that shaped our home.",Cosmic Quests,615
