"How CPU Caches Work: L1, L2, L3, and the Memory Wall Explained","Modern CPUs are incredibly fast, but memory access can be a major bottleneck. This is where CPU caches come into play. They are small, super-fast memory banks designed to store frequently used data and instructions close to the CPU core.

But how exactly do these caches work, and why are there different levels like L1, L2, and L3?

In this deep dive, we explore the intricate world of CPU cache memory, covering:

• The concept of the ""memory wall"" and why caches are essential.
• What are L1, L2, and L3 caches: their size, speed, and placement.
• Cache lines, blocks, and how data is moved between main memory and caches.
• Cache hits and misses: what they are and their performance implications.
• Cache coherency protocols (briefly touching on MESI).
• The principles of temporal and spatial locality.
• How caches impact program performance and how to optimize for them.

Understanding CPU caches is fundamental for anyone looking to optimize software performance, understand computer architecture, or delve deeper into system-level programming.

This video is part of the ""System Performance Deep Dive"" series, building on our previous explorations of CPU pipelines and instruction execution.

Topics Covered:
CPU Cache
L1 Cache
L2 Cache
L3 Cache
Memory Wall
Cache Hierarchy
Cache Hit
Cache Miss
Cache Coherency
Temporal Locality
Spatial Locality
Computer Architecture
Performance Optimization
Memory Management

Subscribe to System Insight for more in-depth explorations of computer systems, performance engineering, and the hidden mechanics of software and hardware.

