QAI-X 2026: Entangled Graph Neural Networks for Quantum Chemistry,"Authors: Lena Petrova, Kai Chen, Dr. Anya Sharma, and Prof. Julian Brandt

Abstract: The simulation of molecular systems on classical computers remains a significant challenge due to the exponential scaling of quantum many-body problems. Quantum Machine Learning (QML) offers a promising avenue to address this. In this work, we introduce Entangled Graph Neural Networks (EGNNs), a novel quantum architecture specifically designed to leverage the inherent entanglement in molecular systems for improved prediction of ground state energies and molecular properties. EGNNs integrate parameterized quantum circuits with classical graph neural network layers, encoding molecular structures as quantum graphs and utilizing entanglement to capture complex correlations. We demonstrate the efficacy of EGNNs on challenging quantum chemistry datasets, including the QM9 dataset and a proprietary dataset of novel drug candidates. Our results show superior performance compared to existing QML approaches, particularly in scenarios requiring high accuracy and generalization. This architecture paves the way for more efficient and accurate quantum chemical simulations, accelerating discovery in materials science and pharmaceutical research.

This presentation was originally given at the Quantum Artificial Intelligence eXchange (QAI-X) 2026 conference in Boston.",Institute for Advanced Quantum Computing,15
