Dental Implants: A Comprehensive Guide to Permanent Tooth Replacement,"A dental implant is a surgical component that interfaces with the bone of the jaw or skull to support a dental prosthesis such as a crown, bridge, or denture. It serves as an artificial tooth root, providing a strong foundation for replacement teeth that look, feel, and function like natural teeth. Dental implants are a popular and effective long-term solution for people who have lost one or more teeth.

Definition and Purpose
Implants consist of a titanium post that is surgically placed into the jawbone, an abutment that connects the post to the crown, and the prosthetic tooth (crown) itself. Their primary purpose is to restore chewing ability, prevent bone loss in the jaw, maintain facial structure, prevent adjacent teeth from shifting, and significantly improve aesthetics and speech. Unlike traditional bridges, implants do not require altering adjacent healthy teeth.

Types of Dental Implants
Endosteal Implants: The most common type, these are placed directly into the jawbone. They are typically shaped like small screws, cylinders, or plates.
Subperiosteal Implants: These implants are placed on top of the jawbone but under the gum tissue. They are an option for patients who cannot wear conventional dentures and have minimal bone height.
Mini Dental Implants (MDIs): Smaller in diameter than traditional implants, MDIs are primarily used to stabilize lower dentures or in situations where bone density is limited.
All-on-4 Dental Implants: A technique where a full arch of teeth can be supported by only four strategically placed implants, offering a full set of new teeth with fewer surgical procedures.",Dental Health Demystified,7
