Why Oil and Water Separate (But Alcohol Blends) | Interfacial Dynamics Simulation,"Everyday liquids interact in fascinating ways — some blend instantly, while others form stubborn layers. This isn't just about 'likes dissolve likes'; it's an intricate dance of molecular forces: polarity, hydrogen bonds, and the ever-present pull of gravity. The invisible boundary where two liquids meet, known as the interface, holds the key.

Interfacial tension is the energy required to expand this boundary, essentially how 'unwilling' two liquids are to mix. Coupled with density differences, these forces dictate whether liquids emulsify, form stable layers, or dissolve completely. High interfacial tension means strong segregation, as seen with oil and water, where non-polar oil molecules are repelled by polar water molecules. Low interfacial tension, like between alcohol and water, allows for easy blending due to similar polarities and hydrogen bonding.

This simulation uses an advanced multi-phase Smoothed Particle Hydrodynamics (SPH) model to visualize the molecular interactions across three scenarios: the stark separation of immiscible oil and water, the seamless blending of miscible alcohol and water, and the dramatic interface between liquid mercury and water, showcasing extreme density and surface tension effects. Watch as particles repel and attract, revealing the fundamental physics behind liquid miscibility.

Try the full interactive simulation: https://simulateitnow.com/mix

#FluidDynamics #Physics #InterfacialTension #MolecularForces #SPH #LiquidMixing #Science #STEM #ScienceEducation #Simulation #Hydrodynamics #FluidMechanics #Interactive #Polarity",Simulated Science Lab,900
