"Projectile Motion - OpenStax University Physics Vol 1, Chapter 4, Problem 82","In this video, I demonstrate how to solve OpenStax University Physics Volume 1, Chapter 4, Problem 82. This problem involves a projectile launched at an angle from the ground, focusing on determining its maximum height, total flight time, and horizontal range. It's an excellent example of applying 2D kinematic equations with initial velocity components.

The core of the solution involves breaking the initial velocity vector into its horizontal (x) and vertical (y) components using trigonometry. We then use the appropriate kinematic equations for constant acceleration in the y-direction (due to gravity) and constant velocity in the x-direction. A common pitfall addressed is ensuring correct sign conventions for acceleration due to gravity and clearly defining the initial and final states for each component of motion.

Topics in this video:
*   Projectile motion fundamentals
*   Decomposing initial velocity into x and y components
*   Using sine and cosine for launch angles
*   Kinematic equations for 2D motion
*   Calculating maximum height of a projectile
*   Determining total flight time
*   Finding the horizontal range of a projectile
*   Understanding independent x and y motion
*   Common errors in projectile motion problems",The Physics Palette,15
