Why Do Golf Balls Have Dimples? | Aerodynamics Explained | #shorts,"Ever wondered why golf balls aren't smooth? 🤔 It's all about aerodynamics! The dimples on a golf ball aren't just for decoration; they play a crucial role in how the ball flies.

When a smooth ball flies through the air, it creates a large area of low pressure behind it, leading to significant drag that slows it down and reduces its flight distance. This is called form drag.

However, the thousands of small dimples on a golf ball create a thin layer of turbulent air around the ball. This turbulent boundary layer actually helps the airflow stay attached to the surface longer, significantly reducing the size of the wake and the pressure drag behind the ball. This allows the golf ball to fly farther and higher than a smooth ball would.

So, next time you hit a golf ball, remember those dimples are doing some serious scientific work to get it to the green! ⛳

#Golf #GolfFacts #Aerodynamics #Physics #EngineeringExplained #SportsScience #DidYouKnow #FactShorts #Shorts #YouTubeShorts #TrendingShorts #ViralShorts #Explore",ScienceUnveiled,87123
