"One Tag, Total Chaos: The Danger of Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)","Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) remains one of the most pervasive and dangerous client-side vulnerabilities in modern web applications. It allows attackers to inject malicious code into web pages, which is then executed by unsuspecting users' browsers.

Unlike SQL Injection, XSS doesn't directly target the database, but instead focuses on the user's browser, enabling a wide range of attacks.

It occurs when a web application fails to properly validate, sanitize, or encode user-supplied input before rendering it back to the browser. This allows an attacker to embed scripts (usually JavaScript) that the browser trusts and executes.

Through XSS, attackers can:

✔ Steal user session cookies, leading to account takeover
✔ Deface websites or inject malicious content
✔ Redirect users to phishing sites
✔ Perform actions on behalf of the user (CSRF)
✔ Deploy malware via drive-by downloads

This vulnerability has impacted countless websites and users, from social media platforms to banking sites.

In this video, you’ll learn:

✔ What Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) is
✔ The different types of XSS (Reflected, Stored, DOM-based)
✔ How attackers inject and execute malicious scripts
✔ Why browsers execute untrusted code
✔ Essential development practices to prevent XSS (input validation, output encoding, Content Security Policy)

Protecting your users from client-side attacks is paramount for web security.

🛡️ Secure your applications.
⭐ Bookmark this video.
🔔 Subscribe for weekly web security insights.",SecuredScripts,150
