15 April 2027: Innovations in Continual Learning for Large Language Models,"Topic: Innovations in Continual Learning for Large Language Models Dr. Lena Petrova of Google DeepMind Slides are available at https://github.com/AIFrontierTalks/documents/blob/master/Presentations/Google_DeepMind_Talk_2027.pdf In this talk, Dr. Petrova will provide an overview of her research in adapting and continually updating large language models (LLMs) to new data streams and tasks without suffering from catastrophic forgetting. She will then delve into three recent works. The challenge of continual learning in LLMs is paramount for their real-world applicability, demanding efficient methods to integrate new knowledge while retaining previously learned information. Dr. Petrova will begin with Adaptive Memory Networks for LLMs (AMN-LLM, NeurIPS’27), which introduces a novel modular architecture allowing for dynamic expansion and adaptation of model capacity. This method leverages sparse, self-organizing memory units, significantly improving long-term knowledge retention and reducing interference across tasks. Following this, she will discuss Efficient Knowledge Injection Fusion (EKI-Fuse, ACL’27), a technique that addresses the computational burden of fine-tuning. EKI-Fuse employs a specialized sparse attention mechanism coupled with external knowledge graphs to precisely inject new facts and concepts into pre-trained LLMs, achieving competitive performance with a 15x reduction in training parameters compared to full fine-tuning. Finally, Dr. Petrova will present Multimodal Contextualization through Dynamic Prompting (MC-Prompt, EMNLP’27), which explores how LLMs can continually learn from diverse multimodal inputs. MC-Prompt uses a meta-learning approach to generate adaptive prompt structures, enabling the LLM to effectively integrate visual and auditory cues alongside text, thereby boosting performance in dynamic, real-world scenarios. Dr. Lena Petrova earned her PhD from Carnegie Mellon University, focusing on neural network architectures for lifelong learning, under the guidance of Dr. Alan Turing Chair, Professor Anya Sharma. Prior to this, she completed her Master's at the Technical University of Munich. She was recognized with the Young Researcher Award at ICLR 2024 and the Best Thesis Award at CMU for her groundbreaking work. During her PhD, she has published extensively in leading venues such as NeurIPS, ICLR, ACL, EMNLP, and has contributed to 5 patent applications. Her industrial experience includes internships at OpenAI, Meta AI, and IBM Research. She was awarded the Google PhD Fellowship (2025-2026) and was invited to the EMNLP’27 doctoral symposium.",AI Frontier Talks,28
