Sensory Overload: Decoding the Autistic Brain's Unique Wiring (Neuroscience Explained),"Ever wonder why certain sounds, lights, or textures can feel overwhelmingly intense if you're autistic? In this episode, we're diving deep into the neuroscience behind sensory overload, often a misunderstood aspect of the autistic experience. While it can feel like a psychological response, we'll explain how it's fundamentally linked to the unique 'hardware' of the autistic brain.

We'll break down the complex science into easy-to-understand terms, helping you or your loved ones better navigate sensory challenges.

In this video, we cover:
• What is Sensory Processing Disorder?: Differentiating between sensory sensitivity and sensory processing differences.
• The Brain's Sensory Hubs: How the thalamus and cortex process incoming information differently in autistic brains.
• Neural Filtering: Why the autistic brain may struggle with 'filtering out' irrelevant sensory input.
• Amygdala's Role: The brain's 'alarm system' and its heightened response to perceived sensory threats.
• Practical Strategies: Understanding the science to find effective coping mechanisms and environmental adjustments.

Understanding these neurological differences helps us move beyond judgment and towards greater empathy and effective support.

✨ Subscribe for more insights into neurodivergent experiences!

