"DEMO Fusion Power Plant Design: Tackling the Engineering Grand Challenges of Materials, Tritium, and Remote Maintenance","This video provides a comprehensive, engineering-focused analysis of the critical challenges involved in designing and operating a DEMO (DEMOnstration) fusion power plant. Building upon decades of research in magnetic confinement fusion, we delve into the next-generation hurdles that must be overcome to bring commercial fusion energy to reality.The presentation is structured into key technical domains, covering: Advanced Materials Science: Addressing neutron damage, extreme heat fluxes, and long-term structural integrity in the first wall and blanket modules. Tritium Fuel Cycle Mastery: Examining the complex processes of tritium breeding, extraction, purification, and safe handling, including inventory management and environmental considerations. Remote Maintenance and Robotics: Detailing the innovative robotic systems and operational strategies required for component replacement and repair within the highly activated core of a fusion reactor. Integrated Plant Operations: Discussing the complex interplay between these challenges and their impact on plant availability, safety protocols, and economic viability.We will explore current research efforts, proposed solutions, and the ongoing R&D required to mature these technologies. The focus is on the integrated system-level impact of these engineering grand challenges and how they drive the overall design and operational strategy of future fusion power plants.Chapters 00:00:00 — Introduction to DEMO and Its Mission 00:06:15 — Extreme Environments and Advanced Materials 00:12:40 — The Tritium Breeding Blanket and Fuel Cycle 00:19:00 — Robotics and Remote Handling for In-Vessel Components 00:25:30 — Safety, Licensing, and Waste Management 00:31:55 — Integrated System Design and Operational Challenges 00:38:10 — Future R&D Pathways and Commercialization Prospects",Fusion Frontiers Engineering,95
