Why Big Cities Get More Rain Than the Countryside,"Have you ever noticed that a storm seems to break right over a major city, while the surrounding rural areas stay dry? It's not just a coincidence.

Cities aren't just hot; they're also active engines for atmospheric change. The towering skyscrapers disrupt wind patterns, while the abundance of heat (thanks to the urban heat island effect) creates updrafts. Add to that the constant release of tiny pollution particles from traffic and industry, and you've got a perfect recipe for rainfall. These particles act as 'cloud seeds,' providing surfaces for water vapor to condense upon.

This phenomenon means urban areas can experience not only more frequent but often more intense precipitation, impacting everything from local ecosystems to urban drainage systems.

It's a subtle yet powerful testament to how human settlements reshape the very skies above them.

#UrbanWeather #ClimateChange #Cities #Science #Meteorology #UrbanPlanning #Rain #Environment #Geography #DidYouKnow #Explained #ScienceShorts",Urban Unseen,680
