Applied Econometrics: Instrumental Variables (IV) Estimation in R #econometrics,"Instrumental Variables (IV) is a statistical method used to estimate causal effects in observational studies when there is endogeneity, typically due to omitted variable bias or measurement error. It relies on finding an instrument: a variable that affects the treatment but is otherwise unrelated to the outcome, except through the treatment. In R, the ivreg function from the AER package is commonly used for 2SLS (Two-Stage Least Squares) estimation. First, ensure you have the AER package installed and loaded. The basic syntax involves specifying the outcome variable, endogenous variable(s), exogenous covariate(s), and the instrumental variable(s). This video demonstrates how to perform IV estimation, interpret the results, and discusses diagnostics such as the Sargan test for overidentifying restrictions, which helps assess the validity of the instruments. We'll walk through a practical example using a simulated dataset.",EconoMetrics Lab,25
