"Daily Aviation Briefing - Dublin FIR - March 18, 2026","Forecast valid from 06:00 UTC, Wednesday 18 March 2026 until 06:00 UTC, Thursday 19 March 2026.

SIGMETs in effect: Nil.
AIRMETs in effect: Moderate mountain obscuration over western highlands due to low clouds and mist.

Meteorological Situation at 03:00 UTC: High pressure ridge extending across central Ireland, slowly drifting east. Weak frontal system approaching from the west, expected to impact western FIR by late afternoon.

Forecast for Dublin FIR (FL000-FL100):

Winds: Surface winds generally light variable, becoming southerly 5-10 knots by midday. Above 2,000 ft, westerly 15-20 knots, increasing to 25-30 knots in the west by evening.

Visibility: Generally good (10km+), occasionally moderate (5-8km) in mist patches, especially near coastal areas and western hills.

Clouds: Scattered cumulus at 2,500 ft, becoming broken by afternoon, with bases lowering to 1,500 ft in the west. Overcast stratocumulus developing by evening from FL050 to FL080 in the west.

Weather: Isolated light rain showers possible in the west by late afternoon.

Icing: Nil significant.

Turbulence: Light chop below FL050 in areas of developing cumulus. Moderate turbulence possible in western mountainous areas due to terrain interaction with increasing winds.

Outlook for a further 24 hours until 06:00, Friday 20 March 2026: Frontal system progresses eastward, bringing widespread moderate rain and lower ceilings across the FIR. Winds strengthening southerly.

