Linear Algebra — 11.2: Unraveling Singular Value Decomposition (SVD),"Dive deep into Singular Value Decomposition (SVD), one of the most powerful and widely used matrix factorization techniques in modern mathematics and data science. This video breaks down the conceptual underpinnings of SVD, revealing how any matrix A can be decomposed into three simpler, interpretable matrices: U, Σ (Sigma), and Vᵀ, representing orthogonal rotations and scaling.

Learn the geometric interpretation of SVD as a sequence of rotation, scaling, and another rotation that transforms the unit sphere into an ellipsoid. Understand how SVD extends the concept of eigenvalues and eigenvectors to non-square matrices and provides a robust framework for understanding matrix transformations.

Key concepts covered:
• Definition of SVD: A = UΣVᵀ, where U and V are orthogonal matrices and Σ is a diagonal matrix of singular values.
• Geometric intuition: How SVD transforms a unit sphere into an ellipsoid, defining principal axes.
• Relationship between singular values and eigenvalues of AᵀA and AAᵀ.
• Constructing the orthogonal matrices U and V from eigenvectors of AᵀA and AAᵀ.
• Calculating singular values as the square roots of the eigenvalues.
• Applications of SVD: dimensionality reduction (Principal Component Analysis - PCA), image compression, denoising, and pseudoinverse calculation.
• Worked example: Decomposing a 2x3 matrix to derive its U, Σ, and Vᵀ components step-by-step.
• Discussion on the uniqueness and robustness of SVD for various matrix types.
• Visualizing the components: The columns of U as left singular vectors and columns of V as right singular vectors.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
