Session 2 Critical Minerals for Africa: Local Value Chain & Global Demand,"African Mining & Energy Forum 2024

Featuring:

Dr. Lena Mwangi (Energy Economist) moderates a crucial discussion on Africa's pivotal role in the global critical minerals supply chain for the energy transition. Panelists Mr. Kwame Nkrumah (CEO, African Rare Earths Corp.), Ms. Aisha Diallo (Policy Advisor, AU Commission for Energy & Infrastructure), and Prof. Thabo Mokoena (Head of Geology, University of Cape Town) explore the opportunities and formidable challenges in establishing robust local value chains, from extraction to beneficiation.

The panel delves into policy frameworks, investment strategies for processing infrastructure, the integration of environmental and social governance (ESG) standards, and the geopolitical dynamics shaping the future of African critical minerals. Lessons from historical resource exploitation are discussed to chart a sustainable and equitable path forward.

Timestamps:
00:00 Introduction: Africa's Geopolitical Leverage in Critical Minerals
02:15 The Global Scramble: Demand Projections & Supply Gaps
05:00 Beyond Extraction: The Imperative for Local Beneficiation
08:30 ESG Standards & Sustainable Mining Practices
11:00 Financing the Future: Attracting Investment for Processing
14:10 Skills Gap & Education: Building the Workforce of Tomorrow
17:00 Geopolitics of Critical Minerals: Navigating Trade Wars
20:00 Case Study: Cobalt in DRC - Lessons Learned

Key Takeaways:

Strategic Importance: Africa holds vast, underexplored reserves of minerals crucial for the global energy transition, including lithium, cobalt, graphite, and rare earths.

Value Addition Imperative: The focus must shift from merely exporting raw materials to developing local processing and manufacturing capabilities, driving economic diversification and job creation across the continent.

Policy & Governance: Stable, transparent, and investment-friendly policy frameworks, coupled with robust governance, are essential to attract necessary capital and ensure equitable benefit sharing.

Infrastructure & Investment: Significant public and private investment is required for the development of processing facilities, renewable energy for operations, and efficient logistics infrastructure.

