What Happens If You Strap 8 Webcams Together for Planetary Imaging?,"Can you really build a high-performance planetary camera from multiple off-the-shelf webcams? In this video, we're putting that theory to the test!

Inspired by the concept of telescope arrays, I'm attempting to construct a multi-camera setup using eight budget webcams to capture stunning details of planets like Jupiter and Saturn. The idea is simple: more cameras mean more photons collected per second, potentially leading to faster frame rates and higher signal-to-noise ratios, crucial for 'lucky imaging' planetary astrophotography.

In theory, this array could offer a significant boost in data collection compared to a single camera, allowing us to freeze atmospheric turbulence more effectively and resolve finer details on planetary surfaces.

Join me in this ambitious DIY project as we explore:

Modifying standard webcams for long-exposure and prime focus use.
Designing and 3D printing a custom mount for precise co-alignment.
Developing software solutions for synchronized capture and data handling.
The immense challenge of processing gigabytes of raw data from multiple sources.

This is the first episode in a series documenting the build and testing of this 'Webcam Planetary Array.' In upcoming videos, we'll delve into the image processing pipeline, compare its performance against dedicated planetary cameras, and showcase its first light images of our solar system's giants.

If you're fascinated by planetary astrophotography, DIY astronomy projects, or pushing the boundaries of budget equipment, hit subscribe and join the journey!

