FluidFlow E-Scooter - Hydraulic Brake Pad Replacement Guide,"FluidFlow E-Scooter - Hydraulic Brake Pad Replacement Guide Tools required: Allen key set (2mm, 3mm, 5mm), Flathead screwdriver, Brake piston spreader tool (or large flathead), Nitrile gloves, Isopropyl alcohol, Clean lint-free cloth, New hydraulic brake pads (FluidFlow compatible). Operating steps: 1. Preparation: Ensure the scooter is powered off and secured on a stand or in a stable position. Place a clean cloth under the brake caliper to catch any fluid drips. 2. Wheel Removal (if necessary): Depending on caliper access, you may need to remove the wheel. If so, use a 5mm Allen key to loosen the axle nuts/bolts. 3. Caliper Access: Locate the brake caliper. On most FluidFlow models, this is a two-bolt attachment. 4. Remove Old Pads: Use a 2mm Allen key to remove the retaining pin that holds the brake pads in place. Carefully slide out the old brake pads. Note their orientation. 5. Piston Reset: Using a brake piston spreader tool (or a large flathead screwdriver carefully), gently push the brake pistons back into the caliper body. This creates space for the new, thicker pads. Be cautious not to damage the pistons or seals. 6. Clean Caliper: Use isopropyl alcohol and a lint-free cloth to clean any residue inside the caliper. Do NOT spray directly into the caliper; apply to the cloth first. 7. Install New Pads: Insert the new brake pads into the caliper, ensuring they are correctly oriented. Re-insert the retaining pin and tighten it with the 2mm Allen key. 8. Re-attach Wheel (if removed): Re-install the wheel, ensuring all bolts are tightened to manufacturer specifications. 9. Brake Lever Test: Squeeze the brake lever several times until you feel consistent pressure. The pads will self-adjust to the rotor. 10. Final Check: Spin the wheel to ensure the pads aren't rubbing excessively. Perform a short, low-speed test ride in a safe area to confirm brake function before full use.",E-Scooter Mechanics,5
