"The 2,000-Year-Old Computer That Should Be Impossible","In 1901, sponge divers off the coast of the Greek island of Antikythera stumbled upon a shipwreck filled with ancient treasures. Among them was a corroded lump of bronze, gears, and inscriptions that, upon closer inspection, revealed something truly astounding: an intricate mechanical device, a kind of analog computer from over two millennia ago. This video dives deep into the Antikythera Mechanism – the most complex piece of engineering known from the ancient world. We explore its sophisticated gear trains, its ability to predict astronomical positions and eclipses, and the mind-boggling implications it has for our understanding of ancient Greek science and technology. How did they build something so advanced, centuries before similar technology reappeared? Was it a lone genius, or a lost tradition of precision engineering?

