How to Calculate the Equilibrium Constant (Keq) from Gibbs Free Energy (ΔG°) | Chemical Thermodynamics,"Learn how to determine the equilibrium constant (Keq) from standard Gibbs Free Energy (ΔG°) in this comprehensive chemical thermodynamics lesson. Mastering this relationship is crucial for understanding reaction spontaneity and equilibrium conditions. Access premium study guides, practice problem sets, and interactive quizzes for thermodynamics and equilibrium concepts at DrChemExplains.com/chemistry-resources. Book a personalized tutoring session with Dr. Chem for advanced problem-solving strategies and exam preparation at DrChemExplains.com/tutoring. In this General Chemistry 2 tutorial, Dr. Chem Explains will guide you through the calculation of Keq using the fundamental equation linking Gibbs Free Energy and the equilibrium constant: ΔG° = -RT ln Keq Where: ΔG° = standard Gibbs Free Energy change R = ideal gas constant (8.314 J/mol·K) T = temperature in Kelvin Keq = equilibrium constant By the end of this video, you will be able to: • Understand the relationship between ΔG° and Keq • Calculate Keq from a given ΔG° and temperature • Apply these concepts to predict reaction spontaneity and equilibrium positions • Solve common General Chemistry II thermodynamics and equilibrium problems This lesson is perfect for students studying: • General Chemistry 2 • Chemical Thermodynamics and Equilibrium • Spontaneity and Free Energy • College-level chemistry exams Prepare for your upcoming chemistry exams with a clear understanding of how to connect Gibbs Free Energy to the equilibrium constant! #DrChemExplains #Chemistry #GeneralChemistry #ChemicalThermodynamics #EquilibriumConstant #GibbsFreeEnergy #ReactionSpontaneity",Dr. Chem Explains,35
