Stomata - botany bite of the week,"Botanical Bites is on Patreon! If you're interested in supporting what I do, head over to [ImaginaryPatreonLink]. Botany bite of the week is... stomata. Stomata are microscopic pores, typically found on the surface of plant leaves, that regulate gas exchange between the plant and the atmosphere. They are crucial for photosynthesis, allowing carbon dioxide to enter the plant, and for transpiration, releasing water vapor. Each stoma is flanked by two guard cells that control its opening and closing, responding to environmental cues like light, humidity, and CO2 levels. Learn why these tiny structures are vital for a plant's health and survival!",Botanical Bites,310
