Astronauts FLOATING in Space... So why doesn't the ISS drift away? 🤔🛰️ #spacefacts #gravityexplained #orbitalmechanics,"Explanation 👇

It's a common misconception that there's 'no gravity' in space, leading people to wonder why astronauts don't just float off into the cosmos. The truth is, gravity is everywhere – it just gets weaker with distance!

Here's what's really happening:

Gravity is Present: Earth's gravity still extends far into space. At the altitude of the International Space Station (ISS), gravity is about 90% as strong as it is on Earth's surface!
What is Orbit? Astronauts aren't 'floating' in zero gravity; they're constantly 'falling' around the Earth. This is called orbital motion.
Continuous Freefall: The ISS (and everything inside it) is traveling sideways at an incredible speed (about 17,500 mph) while simultaneously being pulled towards Earth by gravity. The forward motion perfectly matches the rate at which it falls, meaning it never hits the ground – it just keeps falling *around* the Earth. This creates the sensation of weightlessness, or microgravity.

So, while it looks like astronauts are simply floating in a void without gravity, they're actually in a perpetual state of falling, expertly balancing Earth's pull with their tremendous speed.

Curious how we get rockets up to those incredible orbital speeds?

