RECOVERY CHECKLIST:

1. Make a backup copy of the damaged Excel file immediately.
2. Work ONLY on the copy, never the original.
3. Move the file to a local drive (e.g., C:\) if it's on a network drive or cloud sync folder.
4. Ensure no one else has the file open.
5. Open Excel first, then use File > Open > Browse > Select your file > Click the dropdown next to the 'Open' button > Choose 'Open and Repair'.
6. If 'Open and Repair' fails to fix the file, try selecting 'Extract Data' from the same dialog.
7. Try opening the file in Excel Safe Mode (hold Ctrl while launching Excel, or type 'excel.exe /s' in Run).
8. If the file opens, immediately save it under a new name and/or a different format (e.g., .xlsb, .csv, .xlsx). This can sometimes clean up internal corruption.
9. Restore a previous version of the file using Windows File History or system restore points if available.
10. Check if the file is 'blocked' by Windows (right-click the file > Properties > General tab > look for an 'Unblock' checkbox).
11. Disable automatic calculations and macros upon opening (hold the Shift key while opening the file).
12. If only specific sheets are corrupted, try opening the file with external links only (File > Options > Trust Center > Trust Center Settings > External Content > disable options).
13. Try importing data into a new blank workbook (Data tab > Get Data > From File > From Workbook).
14. Use third-party Excel recovery tools as a last resort, but proceed with caution.
15. If the data is critical and other methods fail, consider a professional data recovery service.",Data Rescue Lab,1150
